# Terminology

The words this project uses, and what they mean here. They are definitions, not
descriptions: code, comments and documents use them with exactly these meanings.

: running prompt

  The thing that runs. It forks, or spawns another, by assembling a prompt, and it
  recalls experience from the world model rather than containing it.

: prompt

  Every chat turn visible to the LithosAI API. Anything the API cannot see is not
  part of the prompt.

: last prompt

  The last turn.

: world model

  The whole of what has been experienced — global, and unique as one. See
  `research/agent-world.dj`.

## A word we do not define

`agent` is fine to say to outsiders, where it is the industry word. It is not a
term here, and not because it is vague: its default meaning is an *owner* — a thing
with its own knowledge, its own state, and its own lifetime. This project denies
exactly that, since experience is not anyone's and a running prompt recalls rather
than contains. So the word asserts something untrue rather than something
imprecise.

The tell is a possessive: "the agent's turns", "the agent's state", "its files".
Wherever one of those appears, the word has put an owner back. Name the thing
instead — the runner is a `running prompt`, the turns are the `prompt`, and what
has been experienced is the `world model`.

## Constraints

- All run1 processes on a machine share a single Linux process group, so the
  machine's whole set of running prompts can be signalled as one.